<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mishneh Torah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1170">1170</a>–<a href="/years/1180">1180</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Sefer Yad ha-Hazaka, Book of the Strong Hand</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mishneh Torah is a code of Rabbinic Jewish religious law (halakha) by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a>, compiled between 1170 and 1180 CE while he was living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and regarded as his magnum opus. Written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and divided into fourteen books, it is the only medieval-era work that details all of Jewish observance, including the laws that apply only when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> is in existence. Maimonides intended a complete statement of the Oral Law, so that a reader who had mastered the Written Torah and this code would need no other book. Early critics objected to its absence of sources and feared it would supersede study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a>, but it remains an important work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mishneh-torah.jpg" alt="Mishneh Torah" width="330" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maimonides (text); Isaac (copist), Matteo di Ser Cambio (illumination) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rambam_mishne-torah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
